artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the family of diluted dipolar-coupled Ising magnets obtained by randomly substituting nonmagnetic Y ions for magnetic rare-earth ions in the following crystalline hosts:

(a) LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4, in which Ho^{3+} ions occupy a tetragonal lattice with strong crystal-field anisotropy producing an effective Ising doublet, coupled by long-range magnetic dipole interactions and weaker exchange, with hyperfine coupling to the Ho nuclear spin and an applied transverse magnetic field producing a tunable transverse-field Ising model (TFIM);

(b) the diluted spin-ice pyrochlores (Dy_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and (Ho_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7, in which the magnetic ions occupy a pyrochlore lattice with local <111> Ising anisotropy and dipolar interactions giving rise to the spin-ice ground-state manifold;

(c) the rare-earth hydroxides Ho_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 and Dy_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3, proposed as TFIM-realizing materials with Ising-like crystal-field doublets and dipolar interactions on a hexagonal lattice.

In each case let H = -\tfrac{1}{2}\sum_{i\ne j} \epsilon_i \epsilon_j J_{ij}^{zz} \sigma_i^z \sigma_j^z - \Gamma \sum_i \epsilon_i \sigma_i^x, where \epsilon_i \in \{0,1\} are i.i.d. Bernoulli(x) site occupations, J_{ij}^{zz} encodes the (anisotropic, long-range) effective Ising couplings appropriate to the host lattice and crystal-field projection, and \Gamma is a transverse field (or effective tunneling generated by hyperfine/CF mixing).

Problem: Determine, for the highly diluted regime x \to 0 of LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4, the nature of the low-temperature collective state (e.g.\ spin-glass, antiglass, ferromagnet, or paramagnet) and identify the mechanism producing the experimentally reported anomalous behavior. In particular, decide whether a comparative analysis — using the dilute pyrochlore Ising magnets (Dy_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and (Ho_xY_{1-x})_2Ti_2O_7 and the dilute hexagonal TFIM candidates Ho_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 and Dy_xY_{1-x}(OH)_3 as control systems with differing lattice geometries, single-ion anisotropies, and hyperfine couplings — suffices to isolate which microscopic ingredient (lattice geometry, sign/anisotropy of dipolar couplings, off-diagonal dipolar terms, hyperfine interaction, random-field generation by the transverse field, or rare-earth-specific crystal-field structure) is responsible for the highly dilute behavior of LiHo_xY_{1-x}F_4.
